 activated thrombin, forming a stable complex which has the effect of inactivating thrombin protease activity (Church et al. 1985), although with slower kinetics than SERPINC1 (antithrombin-III). The presence of the glycosaminoglycans heparin or dermatan sulphate increases thrombin inactivation 1000-fold (Van Deerlin & Tollefsen 199) by facilitating the interaction between the active site of thrombin and the reactive site of SERPIND1. Thrombin specificity is conferred by a 90-residue N-terminal extension that contains two acidic motifs containing sulphated Tyr residues, resembling the C-terminus of hirudin (Tollefsen et al. 1997). SERPIND1 also inhibits chymotrypsin and neutrophil cathepsin G, but in a glycosaminoglycan independent manner (Church et al. 1985). In contrast to SERPINC1 deficiency, SERPIND1 deficiency is not associated with venous thrombosis (Corral et al. 2004).", "literatureReference": [5578886, {"dbId": 5580234, "displayName": "Heparin cofactor II", "title": "Heparin cofactor II", "journal": "Adv. Exp. Med. Biol.", "pages": "35-44", "pubMedIdentifier": 9433487, "volume": 425, "year": 1997, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/9433487", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 5580235, "displayName": "Homozygous deficiency of heparin cofactor II: relevance of P17 glutamate residue in serpins, relationship with conformational diseases, and role in thrombosis", "title": "Homozygous deficiency of heparin cofactor II: relevance of P17 glutamate residue in serpins, relationship with conformational diseases, and role in thrombosis", "journal": "Circulation", "pages": "1303-7", "pubMedIdentifier": 15337701, "volume": 110, "year": 2004, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/15337701", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 5578878, "displayName": "Heparin-catalyzed inhibitor/protease reactions: kinetic evidence for a common mechanism of action of heparin", "title": "Heparin-catalyzed inhibitor/protease reactions: kinetic evidence for a common mechanism of action of heparin", "journal": "Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A.", "pages": "5460-4", "pubMedIdentifier": 6577437, "volume": 80, "year": 1983, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/6577437", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}, {"dbId": 5578879, "displayName": "The N-terminal acidic domain of heparin cofactor II mediates the inhibition of alpha-thrombin in the presence of glycosaminoglycans", "title": "The N-terminal acidic domain of heparin cofactor II mediates the inhibition of alpha-thrombin in the presence of glycosaminoglycans", "journal": "J. Biol. Chem.", "pages": "20223-31", "pubMedIdentifier": 1939083, "volume": 266, "year": 1991, "url": "http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/1939083", "className": "LiteratureReference", "schemaClass": "LiteratureReference"}], "className": "Summation", "schemaClass": "Summation"}], "reviewStatus": {"dbId": 9821382, "displayName": "five stars", "definition": "externally reviewed", "name": ["five stars"], "className": "ReviewStatus", "schemaClass": "ReviewStatus"}, "isChimeric": false, "systematicName": "SERPIND1 binds activated thrombin (factor IIa)", "category": "binding", "input": [{"dbId": 5578885, "displayName": "SERPIND1 [extracellular region]", "stId": "R-HSA-5578885", "stIdVersion": "R-HSA-5578885.1", "maxDepth": 1, "name": ["SERPIND1", "Heparin cofactor 2", "HEP2_HUMAN"], "speciesName": "Homo sapiens", "consumedByEvent": [5578883], "species": 48887, "endCoordinate": 499, "referenceType": "ReferenceGeneProduct", "startCoordinate": 20, "className": "Protein", "inDisease": false, "schemaClass": "EntityWithAccessionedSequence"}, {"dbId": 156786, "displayName": "activated thrombin (factor IIa) [extracellular region]", "stId": "R-HSA-156786", "stIdVersion": "R-HSA-156786.2", "maxDepth": 2, "name": ["activated thrombin (factor IIa)", "FIIa"], "speciesName": "Homo sapiens", "catalystActivities